I have been a little out of the nethack loop for a while, but I recently 
got the hankering to make a new patch.
I've decided to work on an idea that has been kicked around a lot: making 
some of the crappier artifacts more desirable. I did a quick review in 
Google groups to see what has been suggested in the past and also came up 
with a few ideas myself. So here are some thoughts, old and new:

(BTW, I'm not counting Magicbane as a bane here. It's already good 
enough.)

There are two main problem with the various banes:
	1. They are only worth anything at all in very limited situations. 
Most of the monsters they are good against are only dangerous in the 
early game, or appear infrequently. Why use Ogresmasher even against the 
occasional ogre when Firebrand or Stormbringer do a better job?
	2. Even against the monsters they're made for, they're not that 
great. Double damage is only marginally useful until you spend enchant 
weapon scrolls and skill slots. And who is going to do that for a weapon 
that is hardly ever useful?

The easiest way to make these weapons a viable choice for players is to 
1) give them abilities that make them useful in more general situations, 
and 2) make them more effective without needing the investment of scrolls 
and skill slots.

Some ideas for powers that would make the weapons more useful in general 
situations:

For all banes: warning for target monster (like Sting for orcs).

Demonbane: grant level drain resistance
Sunsword: chance to blind opponent, increase light range to the same as a 
lamp
Giantslayer: grant 25 strength? (I don't really like this idea because I 
think that the banes shouldn't give the powers of the monsters they 
fight, but I can't think of anything else.)
Dragonbane: reflection. I don't think this is unbalancing given that the 
player must wield the weapon.
Ogresmasher: grant 18/** strength? (Again, I'm not crazy about this 
idea).
Trollsbane: regeneration? (same objections)
Werebane: (already prevents lycanthropy). Negates effects of new moon?

Some ideas for making the weapons more effective against their target 
monster class:

For all banes:
Change the +d5 hit bonus to +d8, and the damage bonus from x2 to +d12 or 
more. I think +d20 would not be unbalancing. (I'm too lazy to crunch the 
numbers right now, but the idea is that against the target monster class, 
the bane should be marginally better than the best general purpose 
artifact.)
Chance to stun, confuse or cancel (like Magicbane).
Maybe a small chance for instakill.

Demonbane: prevent demon's gating ability (not summon nasties though - 
that's a magical rather than demonic power).
Giantslayer: something to do with "seven in one blow". Maybe if the 
player is surrounded by giants, s/he has a small chance to kill them all 
with one hit.
Trollsbane: prevent troll resurrection. (I know some people like trolls 
to come back, but they can always use a different weapon.)

More tweaking: 

It's been suggested to change Dragonbane to a lance and Giantslayer to a 
two-handed sword. I like these ideas, but for this patch I want to avoid 
breaking bones/save file compatibility.

Grimtooth could be poisoned. This would help out players in the early 
game, while being not very useful in the later game (since your tougher 
monsters are generally poison resistant).

Vorpal Blade needs to be better, given that it is a crowning gift. The 5% 
instakill doesn't make up for the low amount of damage it does. Changing 
the damage bonus from +d1 to +d8 would put it in the same league as 
Snickersnee which seems about right.

The Tsurugi of Muramasa needs to be better. Changing the damage bonus to 
x2 from +d8 would put it in the running with Grayswandir and the Brands, 
which is where it should be. Even if double damage makes the Tsurugi more 
powerful then the others, I think that the fact that it is a two-handed 
weapon balances that out.

Anyway, tell me what you think. I especially would like some more ideas 
about abilities that make the banes more desirable to have in general 
situations (like Sunsword's light source capability). 

I'll try to get started on the patch soon.

